  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Tonga Health Sector Support Program Phase 3, improved health systems preparedness and response capabilities in Tonga. This included support for clinical governance; procurement of essential equipment; and strengthened corporate services including to the other islands. Crucially, a sewerage and water system assessment at the main hospital was undertaken in response to infection control concerns and remedial works began to address hygiene and sanitation concerns.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Tonga Health Sector Support Program Phase 3, improved the quality and availability of essential health services in Tonga. This included the first phase to rollout the Package of Essential Health Services across the country. Australia completed the accessibility audit as part of the expansion of Mu&#x2019;a health clinic and clinical services. Specialist Technical Assistance contributed to outreach services and diagnostic capabilities, including in pathology. Capacity was also developed in data management, PFM, infection prevention and control and technical clinical services.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Continuing to deepen Government of Tonga(Ministry of Health)/DFAT/Tonga Health Promotion Foundation relationship.  Outcomes include establishment of mentoring system with MoH Medical Superintendent in Clinical governance and risk and incident management which has allowed for more formal processes for investigating and managing clinical incidents.  Support via Australian consultant Pathologist for the laboratory has also been put in place as MoH has no fulltime pathologist.   Through this assistance the Pathologist has managed to clear backlog of samples from COVID-19 pandamic.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In early 2025, Tonga faced a significant dengue outbreak that posed serious public health risks, especially to children and vulnerable groups. The rapid spread of the disease placed pressure on the Ministry of Health&#x2019;s capacity to respond, particularly in remote and outer island communities. With Australian Government funding, the Ministry of Health mobilised targeted community outreach and awareness campaigns across Tongatapu and Vava&#x2019;u. Existing outreach platforms&#x2014;such as schools, churches, and community networks&#x2014;were leveraged to deliver integrated public health messages on dengue prevention. Risk communication and community engagement activities ensured consistent messaging on symptoms, prevention, and control. In total, more than 52,000 people (50% female) were reached through these specialised outreach efforts &#x2013; around half of Tonga&#x2019;s population. The coordinated response strengthened community awareness and empowered individuals with knowledge to prevent further transmission. &#x201c;It was a wonderful initiative for us as we were able to preach the awareness on dengue to the public as it [was] a Public Health Crisis,&#x201d; said Dr. Siale Hausia, Chief Medical Officer, Ha&#x2019;apai. The outreach proved vital in containing the outbreak and reinforcing trust in public health systems.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
